## 6. Settings pages actually persist (`app/app/settings/page.tsx`)

Right now `handleSave` just fires a toast — `name`, `email`, `bio`, `publicProfile`, `nseKey`, `brokerKey`, `emailNotifs`, `drawdownAlerts` are all local `useState`, initialized once from the static `CURRENT_USER` mock constant, and thrown away on refresh or on navigating away and back.

1. Add a `profile` slice to `lib/store.ts`'s `useSession` (or a new adjacent `useProfile` store — either is fine, but keep it in the same persisted-session family so it round-trips through `localStorage` the same way `plan`/`credits`/`theme` already do):
   ```
   profile: { name: string; email: string; bio: string; publicProfile: boolean; initials: string }
   updateProfile: (patch: Partial<SessionState['profile']>) => void
   ```
   Seed the initial value from `CURRENT_USER` (same source as today), so nothing visibly changes on first load.
2. Also add `apiKeys: { nseKey: string; brokerKey: string }` and `notificationPrefs: { emailNotifs: boolean; drawdownAlerts: boolean }` to the same slice (these map onto the two other sections already on this page) with a matching updater each, or one combined `updateSettings(patch)` — your call, just make sure it's one coherent shape, not three parallel ad hoc pieces of state.
3. Rewire `SettingsPage` to read initial field values from these new store fields instead of `CURRENT_USER` directly, and make `handleSave` call the updater(s) with the current local draft state before toasting success. Local `useState` for the form fields while editing is still correct (you don't want every keystroke committed to the store) — the fix is that *Save* must actually write through to persisted state, not just show a success toast over nothing.
4. Update every other place that currently reads `CURRENT_USER.name` / `CURRENT_USER.email` / `CURRENT_USER.initials` directly to read from the new store instead, so a changed name/initials actually shows up everywhere: `components/app/app-topbar.tsx` (`AccountMenu`'s name/email row, the `Avatar` initials), `app/app/page.tsx`'s "Welcome back, {name}" banner. `CURRENT_USER` in `mock/data.ts` can stay as the *seed* value — just stop reading it live from more than one place.
5. `app/signup/page.tsx` currently collects a "Full Name" field and throws it away. Once the profile store exists, wire signup's submit handler to call `updateProfile({ name })` with the entered value before redirecting, so a new account actually shows the name the person typed instead of always showing "Neeraj Sharma".

